      {
        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 15,
        "titulo": "The home: «da», the floors and «al terzo piano»",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: «la camera da letto» is the bedroom, and the preposition «da» says what the room is for: a room for sleeping. The same «da» is in «la sala da pranzo» (the dining room). «La camera di letto» is wrong. STEP 2: Italian floors are counted from the ground: «il piano terra» is the ground floor, «il primo piano» is the first floor above it, and so on. In American English the ground floor is the «first floor», so the Italian «primo piano» is the American «second floor». British English uses «ground floor» and «first floor» like Italian. STEP 3: to say which floor you live on, use «a» and the article: «Abito al terzo piano» (I live on the third floor). STEP 4: «al» is «a» + «il». STEP 5: «l'ascensore» is masculine. The article «il» is elided before a vowel: «l'ascensore». STEP 6: «accanto a» means «next to» and works like «davanti a»: «accanto alla cucina».",
        "comparacao": "English joins the words into one compound («bedroom»), so the learner has to choose a preposition in Italian and often picks «di». Italian says what the room is for with «da»: «camera da letto». English says «on the third floor» with «on», and an English speaker says «sul terzo piano». Italian uses «a»: «al terzo piano». American English counts the ground floor as «the first floor». Italian does not.",
        "exemplos": [
